# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# credmgr — deny-by-default secret hygiene
#
# This file is the SECOND line of defence, not the first. The first is that the
# vault does not live here at all: lib/paths.js resolves it to the platform's
# user-data directory (~/Library/Application Support/credmgr, %APPDATA%\credmgr,
# ~/.local/share/credmgr). A path outside the repository is a guarantee; a
# .gitignore is only a promise.
#
# The third line is scripts/guard-no-secrets.mjs, installed as a pre-commit
# hook by `npm run hooks:install` and run in CI. It blocks the commit.
#
# If you are adding a new file type that could ever hold a secret, add it here
# FIRST and commit that change on its own.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── The vault itself (belt and braces — it should never be in-tree) ─────────
.credmgr/
store/
backups/
archive/
*.age
*.tar.age
vault.sha256

# ── Key material, any shape, anywhere ──────────────────────────────────────
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.keystore
*.jks
*.kubeconfig
*.asc
*.gpg
id_rsa*
id_dsa*
id_ecdsa*
id_ed25519*
*_rsa
*_ecdsa
*_ed25519
!*.pub.example

# ── Environment files ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.sample
!.env.template

# ── Cloud / service credential files ───────────────────────────────────────
credentials.json
service-account*.json
gcp-key*.json
firebase-adminsdk-*.json
aws-credentials*
.npmrc
.netrc
secrets.yaml
secrets.yml
secrets.toml
*.mobileprovision

# ── Wallet / chain material ────────────────────────────────────────────────
*mnemonic*
*seed-phrase*
*seedphrase*
keystore/
wallets.json
**/receive-addresses-*.json

# ── Plaintext credential dumps people accidentally create ──────────────────
*credential*.txt
*credentials*.md
*password*.txt
*secret*.txt
*token*.txt
*.local
*.rtf

# ── Build / tooling / OS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
node_modules/
dist/
build/
coverage/
*.log
npm-debug.log*
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.idea/
.vscode/
!.vscode/extensions.json
*.swp
*~

# ── Release and build output (Rust, Tauri, SvelteKit, scripts/release.sh) ──
# `dist/`, `build/` and `node_modules/` are above. v2/.gitignore repeats the Rust ones for anyone
# working inside v2/; this block is what protects a root-level `git add`.
target/
.svelte-kit/
*.app/
*.dmg
*.dSYM/
*.tar.gz
*.tgz

# ── Python build artifacts (tools/docgen) ───────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.venv/
venv/

# ── Local agent / editor state (not secrets, just not for git) ─────────────
.codemap/
.remember/
.planning/
.cleanup_state/

# ── Local agent context, not product documentation ─────────────────────────
# CLAUDE.md describes the maintainer's local three-location layout (live vault /
# public source / V2 design) and their machine's paths. Useful to an agent working
# here; meaningless and mildly revealing to anyone else. It stays on disk and out
# of the repository.
CLAUDE.md
.claude/
# Two files under .claude/ (settings.json and hooks/session-start.sh, the Claude Code on the web
# setup hook) are tracked on purpose; git keeps tracking a tracked file despite the rule above.
# Other agents' local context and scratch state get the same treatment as CLAUDE.md:
AGENTS.md
GEMINI.md
.cursor/
.cursorrules
.windsurf/
.codex/
.aider*
.superpowers/
graphify-out/

# ── Repo-root additions ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Standalone histories + local agent context, kept on disk, never published.
.local-history/

# ── Automated build runtime state (logs and control files, not source) ─────
v2/.autobuild-logs/
.autobuild-stop
.autobuild-needs-human
v2/.build-complete
v2/.migration-expected
.autobuild.lock

# ── Visualization-engine scratch and per-run timing churn (tools/) ──────────
# _sample_out/: upstream build scratch that rode in with the port (untracked).
# evidence/validation/*.json carries a wall-clock render_ms that changes every
# run, so a plain `npm test` would otherwise always leave the tree dirty.
tools/docgen/_sample_out/

# docgen renders of the v2 design corpus. The JSON siblings ARE committed — they are the
# machine-readable artifact (sections, figures, and each document's outbound document/ADR/code
# references), ~3KB each and diffable. The HTML is not: it is a pure render of the committed
# Markdown, and 91% of each file is base64 font data that would be duplicated across all 24
# documents for roughly 5MB of identical bytes. Regenerate any of them with
#   python3 -m tools.docgen.cli build v2/docs/<doc>.md --formats html,json
# docs/blueprints/*.html stays tracked deliberately — one file, and it is the pipeline's own
# proof-of-work artifact rather than a bulk render.
v2/docs/*.html

# ── Public-export output ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# tools/public-export builds the public tree OUTSIDE this repository and refuses an output directory
# inside it. These entries are only a backstop, so a tree, a report or a rejected tree can never be
# committed back by accident if someone points the tool at a path in here.
/public-export-out/
/.public-export/
*.export-report.json
*.REJECTED-*/
